<h3>Five Architectural Dimensions</h3>
<p><strong>Dimension 1: Compute Model</strong> — How does your application run? Containers, serverless functions, managed services, or custom hardware.</p>
<p><strong>Dimension 2: Data Flow</strong> — How does data move between components? Synchronous APIs, asynchronous queues, batch processing, or streaming.</p>
<p><strong>Dimension 3: Resilience</strong> — What happens when things fail? Automatic failover, circuit breakers, graceful degradation, or manual intervention.</p>
<p><strong>Dimension 4: Cost Model</strong> — How do you pay? Fixed infrastructure costs, per-request costs, hybrid, or capacity reservations.</p>
<p><strong>Dimension 5: Operational Complexity</strong> — How much infrastructure do you manage? Fully managed, partially managed, or self-managed.</p>

<div class="why-matters">
<h4>🌍 Why This Matters</h4>
<p><strong>In Startups:</strong> Serverless and managed services reduce operational burden, letting small teams focus on product. Pay-per-use aligns costs with usage.</p>
<p><strong>In Scale-ups:</strong> Containerization and orchestration enable efficient resource utilization. Infrastructure as code enables rapid iteration.</p>
<p><strong>In Enterprises:</strong> Complex governance requirements demand layered architecture, multiple approval gates, and comprehensive audit trails.</p>
</div>

<div class="key-takeaway">
<h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
<ul>
<li>No single architecture is optimal for all use cases—trade-offs are inevitable</li>
<li>Design for your constraints: cost, latency, team size, operational complexity</li>
<li>Build in observability from day one: you cannot manage what you cannot measure</li>
<li>Prioritize reliability over features: one hour of downtime costs more than months of development</li>
<li>Document your architecture decisions and constraints for future teams</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="quiz-box">
