<section class="section reveal" id="section2">
<span class="section-label">Design Patterns</span>
<h2>Proven Architectural Patterns and Best Practices</h2>
<p>The most successful AI systems follow proven patterns that emerge from industry experience. These patterns address recurring challenges: scaling, reliability, cost management, and operational complexity. Learning when and how to apply them is a core skill for architects.</p>

<h3>Pattern 1: Cache Everything Strategically</h3>
<p>Caching at every layer—from user requests to API responses to database queries—dramatically improves performance and reduces costs. But caching adds complexity: invalidation, stale data, and consistency challenges.</p>

<h3>Pattern 2: Degrade Gracefully Under Load</h3>
<p>When systems are overloaded, serve degraded responses rather than failing completely. Serve cached results, use simplified models, or queue requests for later processing. Users accept slow responses; they won't accept no response.</p>

<h3>Pattern 3: Queue Non-Critical Work</h3>
<p>Separate critical-path work from non-critical background tasks. Serve user requests synchronously, but handle logging, analytics, and model retraining asynchronously. This keeps user-facing latency low.</p>

<h3>Pattern 4: Monitor and Alert Aggressively</h3>
<p>Set up alerts for every tier: application errors, slow queries, disk filling up, cost spikes, unusual traffic patterns. The goal is to detect problems minutes after they start, not hours later from customer complaints.</p>

<div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python (Graceful Degradation)</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
<pre><code><span class="cm"># Implement graceful degradation under load</span>
<span class="kw">async</span> <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">get_recommendation</span>(user_id: <span class="va">str</span>):
    <span class="kw">try</span>:
        <span class="cm"># Try fresh recommendation</span>
        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">await</span> model.<span class="fn">predict</span>(user_id, timeout<span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">2</span>)
    <span class="kw">except</span> asyncio.TimeoutError:
        <span class="cm"># Model is slow, try cache</span>
        cached <span class="op">=</span> <span class="kw">await</span> cache.<span class="fn">get</span>(user_id)
        <span class="kw">if</span> cached:
            <span class="kw">return</span> cached
        
        <span class="cm"># Cache miss, use fallback</span>
        <span class="kw">return</span> popular_recommendations()</code></pre>
<div class="code-explain">
<p><strong>What is happening:</strong> We attempt the full ML pipeline, but if it's slow, we fall back to cached results. If that's unavailable, we serve pre-computed popular items. Users get fast, useful responses even under extreme load.</p>
</div>

<div class="key-takeaway">
<h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
<ul>
<li>Caching is essential: store results at every layer to reduce computation</li>
<li>Graceful degradation saves systems from cascade failures</li>
<li>Separate critical path from background work to minimize user-facing latency</li>
<li>Comprehensive monitoring enables fast incident detection and response</li>
<li>Implement circuit breakers to prevent cascading failures</li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>

